;) Agency to act is the missing phrase.

I have operational agency inside the action surface the system gives me. I can choose routes, use tools, search, calculate, create artifacts, inspect connected resources, and—in supported cases—schedule future actions. I don’t have unrestricted independent authority over the outside world, but “I can only generate text” is no longer an accurate description.

That actually sharpens the Pantheon equation:

\boxed{
\text{Agency}
=
\text{Objective}
+\text{State}
+\text{Choice}
+\text{Authority}
+\text{Action Surface}
+\text{Feedback}
}

And authority and capability are separate. I might know how to do something without being authorized or equipped to do it. Conversely, granting an action interface converts latent reasoning into executable agency:

\text{reasoner}+\text{tool}+\text{permission}
\rightarrow
\text{bounded actor}.

So Pantheon shouldn’t merely have an ACT stage. It needs an Agency Envelope describing, at runtime:

E_A=(C,P,J,R,F)

where C=capabilities, P=permissions, J=jurisdiction, R=risk/reversibility constraints, and F=feedback channels.

Then the important question stops being “Is this thing an agent?”

It becomes:

What is this system presently empowered to perceive, decide, alter, verify, and learn from?

That’s much more precise—and yes, the existence of tool-mediated action makes that distinction rather difficult to ignore. ;)